          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which of the following cell types normally rests in the G₀ phase but can re-ente</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which of the following cell types normally rests in the G₀ phase but can re-enter the cell cycle when stimulated?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Epidermal cells of the skin</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Epidermal cells are labile cells — they divide continuously and do not rest in G₀.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Hepatocytes</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Hepatocytes are stable (quiescent) cells that rest in G₀ but can re-enter the cell cycle for regeneration, provided the tissue scaffold remains intact.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Cardiac myocytes</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Cardiac myocytes are permanent cells that cannot divide once mature. When lost, they are replaced by scar tissue, not new cells.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Bone marrow haematopoietic cells</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Bone marrow cells are labile cells that cycle continuously to replenish blood cells — they do not rest in G₀.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Review the three categories: labile cells (always dividing — epidermis, gut, marrow), stable cells (G₀ but can re-enter the cycle — liver, kidney), and permanent cells (cannot divide — neurons, cardiac muscle)."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. Stable cells like hepatocytes rest in G₀ but retain the ability to proliferate when needed — this is why the liver can regenerate after partial hepatectomy, provided the structural scaffold remains intact."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
