        {
          "duration": {
            "from": 309.5,
            "to": 319.5
          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>What is the most reliable early ultrastructural marker that cell injury has beco</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>What is the most reliable early ultrastructural marker that cell injury has become irreversible?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Cellular swelling (hydropic change)</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Cellular swelling is the earliest sign of reversible injury, caused by Na⁺/K⁺ pump failure. It resolves if the stimulus is removed in time.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Fatty change (steatosis)</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Fatty change is a reversible form of injury in which triglycerides accumulate in hepatocytes. It does not indicate irreversibility.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Large, flocculent densities within mitochondria</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. These electron-dense deposits inside mitochondria are the earliest reliable ultrastructural sign that injury has crossed the threshold to irreversibility.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Nuclear pyknosis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Pyknosis is a late nuclear change that occurs after irreversible injury is already established — it is not the earliest marker.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The key distinction: cellular swelling and fatty change are reversible. The turning point is marked by large, flocculent densities in the mitochondria on EM, followed by nuclear changes (pyknosis → karyorrhexis → karyolysis)."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly. Once large flocculent mitochondrial densities appear on electron microscopy, the cell has passed the point of no return — membrane failure and uncontrolled calcium influx follow."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
                "enableSolutionsButton": true,
                "enableCheckButton": true,
                "type": "auto",
