          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A chronic smoker develops replacement of normal respiratory epithelium with stra</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A chronic smoker develops replacement of normal respiratory epithelium with stratified squamous epithelium in the bronchi. This cellular change is best described as:</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Dysplasia</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Dysplasia involves disordered growth with cytological atypia — pleomorphism, raised nuclear-to-cytoplasmic ratio, and loss of polarity. A simple cell-type switch without atypia is not dysplasia.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Metaplasia</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Metaplasia is a reversible switch from one differentiated cell type to another via stem cell reprogramming. Squamous metaplasia in the smoker's bronchi is the classic example.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Hyperplasia</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Hyperplasia is an increase in cell number, not a switch from one cell type to another.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Hypertrophy</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Hypertrophy is an increase in cell size without cell division — it does not involve a change in cell type.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Remember the five adaptations: atrophy (↓ size), hypertrophy (↑ size), hyperplasia (↑ number), metaplasia (cell-type switch via stem cell reprogramming), and dysplasia (disordered growth with atypia)."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. Metaplasia is reversible if the stimulus (smoking) is removed. However, persistent metaplasia can progress through dysplasia to carcinoma if mutations accumulate — never dismiss it as harmless."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
                "enableSolutionsButton": true,
                "enableCheckButton": true,
