        {
          "duration": {
            "from": 761.875,
            "to": 771.875
          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>On examining a section from a patient with renal artery thrombosis, you observe </p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>On examining a section from a patient with renal artery thrombosis, you observe preserved cellular outlines ('ghost cells') with complete absence of nuclei. This pattern is most consistent with:</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Liquefactive necrosis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Liquefactive necrosis destroys all tissue architecture, leaving a cystic cavity filled with neutrophils. It is characteristic of cerebral infarcts and abscesses.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Coagulative necrosis</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Coagulative necrosis preserves ghost cell outlines through protein denaturation but erases the nuclei. It is the hallmark pattern of infarction in solid organs — kidney, heart, and spleen.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Caseous necrosis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Caseous necrosis produces amorphous eosinophilic debris surrounded by epithelioid macrophages and Langhans giant cells. It is the hallmark of tuberculosis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Fat necrosis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Fat necrosis produces basophilic calcium deposits among ghost adipocytes and is characteristically seen following acute pancreatitis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Apply the three-step approach: (1) architecture — cell outlines are preserved; (2) staining — protein denaturation gives eosinophilic ghost outlines; (3) nuclei — absent. Preserved architecture with absent nuclei = coagulative necrosis."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. Coagulative necrosis is the default pattern in solid organ infarcts. The notable exception is the brain, where infarcts produce liquefactive necrosis due to high lipid and enzyme content."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
